Subject: [PATCH] an aimed wander may name a place, not only a creature

WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS

Nothing could put a party inside a dungeon, and the reason was not distance.

Three mechanisms exist and all three refuse. The clear command refuses outside
an instance (DungeonClearChatActions.cpp) and deliberately has no travel verb -
it clears a dungeon, it does not get you to one. The dungeon finder draws its
list from the random-bot manager, so characters on named roster accounts get an
empty list and never queue; it is also unsteerable, inserting every eligible
dungeon rather than the one asked for. And a quest cannot lead the way either:
measured against the world database, the Deadmines map has ZERO quest_poi rows,
so a held dungeon quest gives the travel machinery no destination inside.

That left walking in, the way a player does. The machinery for it already
existed and was already proven over long distances - but it could only be
pointed at a creature. NewRpgWanderNpcAction's aimed branch, added so a bot
could be SENT to a named NPC, gates on `npcEntry` being non-zero:

    if (data.npcEntry && data.pos != WorldPosition())

An instance portal is not a creature. It is an areatrigger, and the nearest
spawned creature to the one this was written for sits 22.8 yards away - well
outside the trigger's 7 yard radius, so aiming at that creature stops short and
never crosses it. There was no way to say "walk to this spot", because every
target had to be something with a GUID standing on it.

WHAT THIS CHANGES

The gate becomes the POSITION rather than the entry. `pos` is written only by
the two-argument ChangeToWanderNpc, so a wander the bot selected for itself
still carries an empty `pos` and still falls through untouched - the behaviour
of every unaimed bot on the realm is byte for byte what it was. What is newly
expressible is an aim with a position and no entry, meaning: go there, and
arriving is the errand.

The walk itself needed no change at all. It was always positional - MoveFarTo
takes the coordinates and the entry is consulted only on arrival, to find
something to interact with. This patch simply stops requiring that something to
exist.

For the motivating case the arrival branch is usually never reached: the walk
closes to INTERACTION_DISTANCE, the portal's trigger radius is wider than that,
so the bot crosses the trigger and changes map before it can arrive. Where a
place genuinely has nothing at it, idling is the honest answer and upstream's
own status clock releases the aim with no special case.

WHAT WOULD LET THIS PATCH BE DELETED

Upstream growing any way to send a bot to a location rather than to a creature -
a coordinate aim, an areatrigger aim, or a travel verb on the dungeon module.
Any of those makes this redundant. Deleting it without one costs the ability to
enter a dungeon at all; it does not break the compile.

APPLIES TO

mod-playerbots at the pinned SHA, src/Ai/World/Rpg/Action/NewRpgAction.cpp,
NewRpgWanderNpcAction::Execute - specifically the aimed-wander block. Depends
on the aimed-wander block existing, so it must apply after the patch that adds
it.

diff --git a/src/Ai/World/Rpg/Action/NewRpgAction.cpp b/src/Ai/World/Rpg/Action/NewRpgAction.cpp
index acf0082f..26d49f1a 100644
--- a/src/Ai/World/Rpg/Action/NewRpgAction.cpp
+++ b/src/Ai/World/Rpg/Action/NewRpgAction.cpp
@@ -407,11 +407,35 @@ bool NewRpgWanderNpcAction::Execute(Event /*event*/)
     // where it was sent. It is still bounded, and by upstream's own clock: the
     // status self-expires to IDLE after statusWanderNpcDuration, which releases
     // the aim with no special case anywhere.
-    if (data.npcEntry && data.pos != WorldPosition())
+    // A DESTINATION WITH NO NPC NAMED IS A PLACE, NOT A CREATURE. The gate is
+    // the POSITION, not the entry: `pos` is written only by the two-argument
+    // ChangeToWanderNpc, so a wander the bot chose for itself still has an
+    // empty `pos` and still falls straight through, byte for byte as before.
+    // Leaving `npcEntry` 0 asks for the walk WITHOUT the arrival interaction,
+    // which is what "go to this spot" means and what no aim could previously
+    // express - every target had to be something with a GUID standing on it.
+    //
+    // The motivating case is an instance portal. Its areatrigger teleports on
+    // contact, and the trigger radius is wider than the distance this walk
+    // closes to, so a bot aimed at the trigger's own coordinates crosses it and
+    // changes map mid-walk - the arrival branch below is usually never reached
+    // at all. Nothing else can put a party inside a dungeon: the clear command
+    // refuses outside one and does not travel, and the dungeon finder draws its
+    // list from the random-bot manager, which roster characters are not in.
+    if (data.pos != WorldPosition())
     {
         if (bot->GetDistance(data.pos) > INTERACTION_DISTANCE)
             return MoveFarTo(data.pos);
 
+        // Arriving IS the whole errand when no NPC was named. Idle rather than
+        // hunt for a creature the caller never asked for; the status expires on
+        // upstream's own clock and whoever set the aim decides what is next.
+        if (!data.npcEntry)
+        {
+            info.ChangeToIdle();
+            return true;
+        }
+
         Creature* aimedNpc = bot->FindNearestCreature(data.npcEntry, INTERACTION_DISTANCE * 3);
         if (!aimedNpc || !aimedNpc->IsAlive())
         {
